# Testing Guidelines

As an engineering team, code quality needs to be a priority if our objectives include shipping with as little bugs, making changes without breaking existing behaviour and, delivering these changes as quickly as possible. One way for us to maintain high code quality is by having excellent tests and testing practices. With that, here are our team’s guidelines for writing maintainable and purposeful tests.

<aside>
📖 If you haven’t done so already, we recommend you read through our [Testing Definitions](Testing Definitions.md) reference before continuing through this page.

</aside>

## Golden rules

Rules 1 - 6 will help preventing brittle tests

Rules 8 - 12 will help writing clear tests

### 1. Test behaviour rather than implementation 👔

Implementation tests are tightly coupled to the code's structure and may break easily when implementation details change. On the other hand, functional tests are less sensitive to changes in the underlying implementation, making them more robust and easier to maintain over time.

**How**

The key to functional tests is to make setup and expectation (or arrangements and assertions) by leveraging existing code. For example: in order to test the purchase cancellation, we need a properly booked purchase first. 

❌ An implementation test would seed the purchase like this

```tsx
it("should cancel the purchase", async () => {
  const purchase = purchase_factory.build({
		state: "booked",
		cc_last_four_digits: "1234"
	});
	await Purchase.create(purchase);

	await purchase_cancellation.execute(purchase.id, "1234");

  expect(await Purchase.findById(purchase.id)).toHaveProperty("state", "cancelled");
});
```

✅ A functional test would seed the purchase by emulating the previous required steps

```tsx
it("should cancel the purchase", async () => {
  const purchase_id = await bookCart(CheckoutStoryBuilder
		.throughPubweb()
		.payingWithCardViaDropin({
	    card_number: "4111111111111234"
	  })
    .build()
	);

	await purchase_cancellation.execute(purchase_id, "1234");

	const purchase = await purchase_repository.getById(purchase_id);
	expect(purchase.isCancelled()).toBeTrue();
});

async function bookCart(checkout: CheckoutStory) {
  const purchase_cart_test = await PurchaseProcessTest.create(checkout);
  const result = await purchase_cart_test.processPurchase();
  result.assertBooked();
  return result.purchase.id;
}
```

There are two disadvantages for the first approach:

1. We are assuming that  `cc_last_four_digits` will be set during purchase creation, if that changes this test won’t fail and the feature will be broken
2. Seeding consistent models can be way more complicated than the above. In this example we only care about the purchase, but it won’t be to long until we’ll have rules around the tickets, departures, payment method, loyalty credits, etc. The best way to seeding complex relationships is by executing the code that creates them

---

### 2. Test state, not interaction

Emphasizing state testing is more scalable; it reduces brittleness, making it easier to change and maintain code over time

**How**

❌ Don’t use `sinon`

```tsx
it("should update my entity", async () => {
  const repository = {save: sinon.stub()};
  const service = new Service(repository);

  await service.execute(my_entity);

  assert.equal(my_entity.state, "new_state");
  sinon.assert.calledWith(repository.save, my_entity);
});
```

✅ Assert based on the new state

```tsx
it("should update my entity", async () => {
  const repository = new Repository();
  const service = new Service(repository);

  await service.execute(my_entity);

	const updated_entity = await repository.findById(my_entity.id);
  assert.equal(updated_entity.state, "new_state");
});
```

🤫Only write interaction tests when it’s not possible to write a functional one. For example, testing an external API can only be done with interaction tests, as we’ll be asserting that “the API is invoked with X headers Y query parameters and Z body”. 

---

### 3. Prefer large scoped tests

Large scoped tests gets you more coverage with less code, as they traverse different layers of the application. 
**How**

1. Test your feature at the highest possible level i.e. test the controller / use case
2. Test public APIs only
    1. Don’t expose methods just for testing sake
    2. If a helper method exists only to support one or two classes it’s functionality should be tested though those classes instead of directly

🤫 Write narrow scoped tests to

1. Battle test specific functions
2. Testing repositories or APIs
3. Covering edge cases that are not possible to setup through large scoped tests
4. Shorten the feedback loop. Large scoped tests won’t always give you the best message to understand what failed

---

### 4. Write sociable tests

There’s no good reason for using test double other than for keeping obeying the restrictions of the given test size.

If you find yourself wanting to use a test double to simplify the test setup invest in rule 5

**How**

❌ Using test doubles to simplify setup

```tsx
const get_passenger_questions = {
   execute: () => Promise.resolve(trip.passenger_questions)
};
const validate_answers = new ValidateAnswers(get_passenger_questions);

validate_answers.execute(trip, passengers);
```

✅ Using test doubles to speedup execution

```tsx
const get_passenger_questions = new GetPassengerQuestions(
	new InMemoryCountryRepository(),
	new NoopLogger()
);
const validate_answers = new ValidateAnswers(get_passenger_questions);

validate_answers.execute(trip, passengers);
```

### 5. Use consistent fixtures

Factories that provide test object should do their best to keep things consistent and realistic. The effect of inconsistent is that implementation changes can break our tests even though it shouldn’t.

❌ Allowing to override some values without overriding the rest

```tsx
buildPrices(params: Partial<PublicPriceGroup["prices"]> = {}): PublicPriceGroup["prices"] {
  return {
    currency: "USD",
    total: 500,
    breakdown: {
      base: 500,
      taxes: 0,
      supplier_fees: 0,
      busbud_fees: 0,
      passengers: [
        {
          category: "adult" as SourcePassengerCategory,
          total: 500,
          breakdown: {
            base: 500,
            taxes: 0,
            supplier_fees: 0,
            busbud_fees: 0
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    ...params
  };
}
const prices = buildPrices({base: 1000});
// base price is 1000
// total price is 500
// sum of passenger prices is 500
```

✅ Update calculated properties to produce a consistent fixture

```tsx
buildPrices(passengers: Partial<PassengerPrices>[], currency = "USD"): PublicPriceGroup["prices"] {
  return buildPricesFromPassengerPrices(
    currency,
    passengers.map((params) => ({
      category: params.category,
      breakdown: {
        base: 250,
        taxes: 0,
        supplier_fees: 0,
        busbud_fees: 0,
        ...params.breakdown
      }
    }))
  );
}
const prices = buildPrices([{base: 1000}]);
// base price is 1000
// total price is 1000
// sum of passenger prices is 1000
```

### 6. Provide tested Test Doubles for infrastructure logic🥸

When working big projects, tests’ execution time is a concern and in order to keep tests size we might incur into unit testing most of our domain logic. To do so, we’d need to use replace some implementations with tests doubles.

Whenever you add an external dependency such as a repository or an API, provide both the real and a fake implementation that can be used in the rest of the tests, without having to explicitly stub the real implementation. 

For example, [`GrandCentralClientFake`](https://github.com/busbud/geo-service/blob/f152e71957f059c1d56332957f36e855ab8ca5f0/src/clients/grand-central/grand-central.fake.ts#L6) is a fake implementation for a `GrandCentral` client. Depending on nature of the dependency it may be possible to easily mimic the real implementation to some extend. Sometimes is not possible at all, and that’s fine, having the fake implementation return / resolve without throwing is good enough. What’s also not desired is to put too much effort in the fake implementation, we don’t want to recreate the service!

Another example are Repositories, usually you can have the `SqlSomethingRepository` or the `RedisSomethingRepository` . For simple repositories with `getById` , `findAll` and `save` methods, an `InMemorySomethingRepository` is fairly simple to do.

In order to increase the fidelity of our test suite is important that we run the same tests for both the real and the fake implementation like in [this example](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/f4b8c2e23274b45c8cead5925172cd581b177f38/apps/bookie/tests/integration/components/user/infrastructure/repository/user-repository-test.ts#L22-L49)

### 7. Use the right test size distribution for the project

Small projects will have a manageable amount of tests and thus can benefit from writing mainly large scoped integration tests. In this case the test distribution should be like the [test trophy](https://kentcdodds.com/blog/the-testing-trophy-and-testing-classifications) 

However big projects emphasize in isolating domain from infrastructure in order to provide fast and deterministic test suite for the business logic. These projects will follow the traditional [test pyramid](https://martinfowler.com/bliki/TestPyramid.html)  

---

### 8. Make tests complete and concise

A test is *complete* when its body contains all of the information a reader needs in order to understand how it arrives at its result.

A test is concise when it contains no other distracting or irrelevant information

❌ Not complete, where is the result coming from? I need to go hunting

```tsx
beforeEach(() => {
	let context = context_factory.build({ amount: 100 });
  let tax_rules = [tax_rule_factory.build()];
});

it("calculates total taxes", () => {
   const result = applyTaxes(tax_rules, context);

   assert.equal(result.total, 9);
});
```

❌ Not concise, too much information

```tsx
it("calculates total taxes", () => {
   const context = {
    amount: 100,
		contract_address: {
      city_id: "43e91439-777a-4315-9299-ad1ae720b6c9",
      country_code3: "CAN",
      region_id: "456"
    },
    trip_origin: {
      city_id: "81449407-544c-43e1-a25e-ee40f2fab5c9",
      country_code3: "CAN",
      region_id: "123"
    },
   };
   const tax_rules = [{
     category: TaxableCategory.Commission,
     condition_expression: "contract_address.country_code3 == 'CAN'",
     eval_expression: "value_amount * 0.09",
     tax_included: false,
     priority: 0,
   }]; 

   const result = applyTaxes(tax_rules, context);

   assert.equal(result.total, 9);
});
```

✅ Contains all and only the relevant information for the test

```tsx
it("calculates total taxes", () => {
   const context = context_factory.build({
    amount: 100,
		contract_address: {
      country_code3: "CAN",
      // Small object values may not deserve a factory
			// but we can still be explicit about their irrelevancy
      city_id: "irrelevant",
      region_id: "irrelevant" 
    },
   });
   const tax_rules = [tax_rule_factory.build({
     condition_expression: "contract_address.country_code3 == 'CAN'",
     eval_expression: "value_amount * 0.09",
   })]; 

   const result = applyTaxes(tax_rules, context);

   assert.equal(result.total, 9);
});
```

### 9. Test behaviours, not methods

Framing tests around methods can naturally encourage unclear tests because a single method might do a few different things under the hood and a single test case might invoke different methods

This closely relates to rule 1 as we don’t want our tests to be tied to implementations details

❌ No need to fit each method within a describe block

```tsx
describre("ItemRepository", () => {
  describe("create", () => {
    it("creates an item", async () => {
      const item = anItem();

      await repository.save(item);

      assert.deepEqual(await MyORM.Item.findById(item.id), item);
		});
  });

  describe("findById", () => {
    it("finds an item", async () => {
			const created_item = await MyORM.Item.create(anItem());

      const item = await repository.findById(created_item.id);

      assert.deepEqual(item, created_item);
		});
  });

  describe("findAll", () => {
    it("finds all items", async () => {
			const created_items = await MyORM.Item.createMany([
       anItem(),
			 anItem(),
       anItem(),
			 anItem(),
      ]);

      const items = await repository.findAll(item);

      assert.deepEqual(items, created_items);
		});
  });
});
```

✅ Test behaviours instead, allow a test to invoke multiple methods

```tsx
describre("ItemRepository", () => {
  it("creates an item", async () => {
    const item = anItem();

    await repository.save(item);

	  const created_item = await repository.findById(item.id);
    assert.deepEqual(created_item, item);
	});

  it("finds all items", async () => {
		const created_items = await Promise.all([
     repository.create(anItem()),
		 repository.create(anItem()),
     repository.create(anItem()),
		 repository.create(anItem()),
    ]);

    const items = await repository.findAll(item);

    assert.deepEqual(items, created_items);
	});
});
```

### 10. **Consider tests as documentation** 📖

---

Tests also act as documentation of what should happen. 

Don’t put logic into tests, it is hard for reviewers to understand what the test is about if it’s [full of code.](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/f4b8c2e23274b45c8cead5925172cd581b177f38/apps/bookie/tests/integration/components/booking-manager/transform-to-purchase-test.js#L343-L366)

```tsx
it("should consider dates between outbound and min as available", () => {
  const outbound_date = "2016-01-14";
  const min_date = "2016-01-10";

  const result = CalendarRoutes.getAvailabilities(outbound_date, min_date);

  // ❌ Bad, I have to execute code in my head to understand it 
  assert.deepEqual(result, [11,12,13].map(day => `2016-01-${day}`);
  // ✅ Good
  assert.deepEqual(result, ["2016-01-11", "2016-01-12", "2016-01-13"]);
});
```

Provide clear failure messages

```tsx
  // ❌ Bad, the failure would be "Expected false to be true"
  assert.isTrue(haystack.includes("needle"));
  // ✅ Good, the failure would be "Expected [] to include 'needle'"
  assert.include(haystack, "needle");
  // ✅ Also good, the message adds relevant information
  assert.isTrue(obj.is_active, "source not active");
```

### 11. **Make the test implementation interchangeable** 📦

---

Consider the following scenario: I’m writing a simple endpoint that creates new entity and saves it to the database. 

The controller and the use case are fairly simple, the logic lives at the repository which is responsible to persisting the entity; but because I followed the “Prefer large scoped tests” guideline my tests operate at the endpoint level.

```tsx
it("should create an item", async () => {
    const item = aItem();

    await saveItem(item);

	  expect(await getItem(item.id)).toEqual(item);
});

it("should update an item", async () => {});

it("should delete an item", async () => {});

function saveItem(item: Item): Promise<void> {
  await app.post("/items", item);
}

function getItem(item_id: string): Promise<Item> {
 return app.get(`/items/${item_id}`, item).then(res => res.body);
}
```

By encapsulating the Arrange / Act / Assert operations I can easily narrow the test’s scope to one that only handles persistence of items. Reviewers will be happy when they see that you are only moving a file and changing a function implementation, rather than the entire test suite! This can be useful when new features are added to the `/items` and we want to isolate them from this behavior

### 12. Avoid nesting when you’re testing

Nesting describe blocks and abusing of `beforeEach` blocks can help reusing setup with the cost of making them not complete. They also behave differently across testing libraries and remembering that is hard.

❌ Don’t nest excessively

```tsx
describe('when username and password is provided', () => {
    beforeEach(() => {...})

    describe('when the submit button is clicked', () => {
      beforeEach(() => { ... })

      it('should call onSubmit with the username and password', () => {...})
    })
  })

  describe('when the password is not provided', () => {
    beforeEach(() => {...})

    describe('when the submit button is clicked', () => {
      beforeEach(() => {...})

      it('should show an error message', () => {...})
    })
  })

  describe('when the username is not provided', () => {
    beforeEach(() => {...})

    describe('when the submit button is clicked', () => {
      beforeEach(() => {...})

      it('should show an error message', () => {...})
    })
  })
```

✅ Keep tests flat

```tsx
describe("Login form", () => {
  beforeEach(commonSetup)
  afterEach(cleanup)

	it('calls onSubmit with the username and password', () => {...})

	it('shows an error message when submit is clicked and no username is provided', () => {...})

	it('shows an error message when password is not provided', () => {...})

  // Sometimes adding these blocks can help organize tests
  // as long as they are used for making them clearer
  // and not to resuse code it's fine, but only 1 level!
  context("when passwordless auth is enabled", () => {...})
})
```

### 13. **Keep tests as near as possible to the code under test** 🤼

---

The guideline here is to keep your tests as close to the code they test. This provides a few benefits:

- can visually define the testing boundary of the tests
- emphasizes the point of your tests serving as documentation and the specification of the code
- tests are easily accessible from the context of the code

With this approach, it is important to still create a distinction between tests and regular code project files. Our convention is to use the `.test.ts` (for integration tests) or `.spec.ts` (for unit tests) suffix for test file names which are then placed in next to the implementation.

Keep test files below 300-400 lines, if it grows bigger split them into separate files by use case. [Example](https://github.com/busbud/geo-service/tree/master/src/features/geoEntity/useCase/createGeoEntity/__tests__)

---

## Symptoms of ineffectiveness

| Symptom | Possible broken rules |
| --- | --- |
| Test fail when you change an implementation detail | → 1. Test behaviour rather than implementation
→ 5. Use consistent fixtures |
| Test fail when you refactor code | → 2. Test state, not interaction |
| Refactoring leads you to update tests | → 3. Prefer large scoped tests
→ 9. Test behaviours, not methods  |
| It takes time to update a test after you change behaviour | → 4. Write sociable tests
→ 11. Make the test implementation interchangeable |
| Adding a test requires lots of setup | → 5. Use consistent fixtures
→ 6. Provide tested Test Doubles for infrastructure logic |
| It takes time to figure out why a test is failing | → 10. Consider tests as documentation
→ 12. Avoid nesting when you’re testing |
| Tests are flaky | →7. Use the right test size distribution for the project |
| Test suite becomes slow | →7. Use the right test size distribution for the project |

---

