# Ancillaries

# Overview

We offer ancillary products that are managed by ~~Hepstar~~. We’re moving away from Hepstar. We have our own insurrance called CFAR.
This documentation may be less relevant.

Ancillary related logic lives in bookie under `components/ancillary`.

<aside>
⚠️ Lots of things missing here, please share your knowledge

</aside>

# Docs

Hepstar’s online documentation is this [https://hepstar2016.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AD/overview](https://hepstar2016.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AD/overview). It provides a high level understanding of the API capabilities.

More detailed docs were uploaded to Google Drive under [Teams/Squad/Payments/Hepstar](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/19TkjAobQ1sGXwMvZR6Z6qUYdHwoFWCZb).

# Disabling Hepstar

In the past we had issues with Hepstar becoming unavailable which prevented purchases from going through. In case that happens again, we can disable Hepstar to allow bookings without ancillaries.

The quickest way to disable Hepstar is through Growthbook, in particular the [hepstar-ancillary](https://growthbook.busbud-int.com/features/hepstar-ancillary) flag.

There you can disable the feature per environment

![](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/5630976/223780135-e9957040-a5f9-4452-9261-7e1b7f0c70ae.png)

<aside>
💡 Keep in mind that if, for whatever reason, the application can’t connect to Growthbook, the flag will also fallback to `HEPSTART_ENABLED` value; so it’s safer to keep these in sync.

</aside>

# Adding a new Hepstar item

We integrate with a partner called Hepstar to offer our users ancillary items, and sometimes Hepstar add support for new items to our account. Here are the steps you should follow to start serving a new item to our users.

## CartItem

When we query Hepstar's server to ask for available products, Hepstar send us an `InternalName` field for each product in their response so we can identify the different items. A new item means a new `InternalName`.

The first step is to add the `InternalName` of the new item to our [ID_MAPPINGS](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/cf6b2db79bc1b7c2fccdf7bacb7ea46f69766c3c/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/infrastructure/client/hepstar/from-response.ts#L21-L23), and create an [ItemDataType](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/cf6b2db79bc1b7c2fccdf7bacb7ea46f69766c3c/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/domain/model/item-data.ts#L4-L7) mapping for the new item:

```tsx
const ID_MAPPINGS = {
  existing_internal_name: ItemDataType.ExistingItem,
  new_internal_name: ItemDataType.NewItem,
} as const;
```

Now make some api requests to Hepstar's sandbox environment and check if the new item is showing up. One way to do this is to run a test from the Hepstar client test file against Hepstar's sandbox environment, then check the actual/expected output diff of the client method test for the new item. There are instructions for how to do this in the [client test file](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/cf6b2db79bc1b7c2fccdf7bacb7ea46f69766c3c/apps/bookie/tests/integration/components/ancillary/infrastructure/client/hepstar/index-test.ts).

If you try this and don't see a new item, either Hepstar's sandbox env isn't serving the new item, or the `InternalName` doesn't match the one you entered. Log the raw response.body from the request, figure out which is the case, and talk to the partner/adjust the `ID_MAPPINGS` as appropriate.

You might also see an error like the following: `UnexpectedResponse: could not parse hepstar priced products response`. We have defined expected response schemas for Hepstar using [zod](https://github.com/vriad/zod), and this error means the new item does not conform to a response schema we defined. You can get more info on what exactly is going wrong by adding the following to the request method before the `schema.check` calls:

```tsx
try {
  priced_products_schema.parse(json_response)
} catch (error) {
  console.log('>>>', JSON.stringify(error, null, 4));
}
```

Ideally, Hepstar should return a single schema for all their items, so you should ask them why the schema for the new item is different from prior items and ask them to update their response to fix this.

If the item is completely different and this isn't possible, you'll need add support for the new response schema in [response-schemas](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/cf6b2db79bc1b7c2fccdf7bacb7ea46f69766c3c/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/infrastructure/client/hepstar/response-schemas.ts) and add support for mapping the response to a cleaner schema in [from-response](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/cf6b2db79bc1b7c2fccdf7bacb7ea46f69766c3c/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/infrastructure/client/hepstar/from-response.ts).

Once you're done with the above steps, update the [ItemData](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/cf6b2db79bc1b7c2fccdf7bacb7ea46f69766c3c/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/domain/model/item-data.ts#L9) type to reflect the new output. Hopefully all you'll need to do is add the new `type` variant to an existing interface:

```tsx
// New item, was able to map new item response schema to existing ItemData schema
// From
export interface ExistingInsuranceItemData {
  type: ItemDataType.ExistingInsuranceItem;
  insurer_code: string;
  ...
}

export type ItemData = ExistingInsuranceItemData;

// To
export interface MoreGenericNameItemData {
  type: ItemDataType.ExistingInsuranceItem | ItemDataType.NewItem;
  insurer_code: string;
  ...
}

export type ItemData = MoreGenericNameItemData;
```

If the new Hepstar response was too unique to map to an existing schema, you'll need to add an entirely new schema variant:

```tsx
// New item, was able to map new item response schema to existing ItemData schema
// From
export interface ExistingInsuranceItemData {
  type: ItemDataType.ExistingInsuranceItem;
  insurer_code: string;
  ...
}

export type ItemData = ExistingInsuranceItemData;

// To
export interface ExistingInsuranceItemData {
  type: ItemDataType.ExistingInsuranceItem;
  insurer_code: string;
  ...
}

export interface NewItemData {
  type: ItemDataType.NewItem;
  product_code: string;
  ...
}

export type ItemData = ExistingInsuranceItemData | NewItemData;
```

`ItemData` is static and bookie doesn't do much more than pass it to clients, but this changes when we convert a `CartItem` to a `PurchasedCartItem` that the user has selected and checked out. The conversion happens [here](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/10cc7728db25b126f88322f6a53e5b110273f610/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/domain/model/purchased-cart-item.ts#L100).

But before moving onto `PurchasedCartItem`s, it will be useful to understand two patterns you'll see throughout the codebase: the `ItemPurchaseData` pattern, and the `assertNever` pattern.

## ItemPurchaseData

`ItemData` type variants are only modelled across one dimension: `type`, but `ItemPurchaseData` type variants are modelled across two dimensions: `type` (item type), and `state` (purchase state). This is somewhat complex and we may try to simplify it in future, but it at least keeps our code typesafe for now.

At time of writing, there are 5 possible states:

- started: your `ItemPurchaseData` has just been converted from `ItemData`.
- failed: you did not complete your booking (e.g. the api call failed, or you decided not to try and book as the main departure booking failed).
- completed: you successfully booked with hepstar.
- cancelled: you successfully cancelled a booking with hepstar.
- cancellation_error: you tried and failed to cancel your booking with hepstar.

As you add support for your new item, for example when converting a cart item to a purchased cart item, the function signatures will require that you modify a few union types. For example the [toItemPurchaseData](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/10cc7728db25b126f88322f6a53e5b110273f610/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/domain/model/purchased-cart-item.ts#L100) function will require you modify the `ItemPurchaseDataStarted` union type before you can add support for your new item. When updating these types, there are two main scenarios: a new item matches a pre-existing schema, and a new item has completely new schemas at certain stages of the purchase flow (as with `ItemData`, we try to avoid the second scenario).

If a new item matches a pre-existing schema, the updated types might look like this:

```tsx
// From
interface CancellationInsuranceStarted {
  type: ItemDataType.CancellationInsurance,
  ...
}
interface CancellationInsuranceFailed {
  type: ItemDataType.CancellationInsurance,
  ...
}

export type ItemPurchaseDataStarted =
  | CancellationInsuranceStarted
...

// To
interface MoreGenericNameStarted {
  type: ItemDataType.CancellationInsurance | ItemDataType.NewItemType,
  ...
}
interface MoreGenericNameFailed {
  type: ItemDataType.CancellationInsurance | ItemDataType.NewItemType,
  ...
}

export type ItemPurchaseDataStarted =
  | MoreGenericNameStarted
...
```

If we cannot avoid defining an entirely new schema, the updated types will look more like this:

```tsx
// From
interface CancellationInsuranceStarted {
  type: ItemDataType.CancellationInsurance,
  ...
}
interface CancellationInsuranceFailed {
  type: ItemDataType.CancellationInsurance,
  ...
}

...

export type ItemPurchaseDataStarted =
  | CancellationInsuranceStarted
  ...

...

// To
interface CancellationInsuranceStarted {
  type: ItemDataType.CancellationInsurance,
  ...
}
interface CancellationInsuranceFailed {
  type: ItemDataType.CancellationInsurance,
  ...
}

interface NewItemStarted {
  type: ItemDataType.NewItemType,
  ...
}
interface NewItemFailed {
  type: ItemDataType.NewItemType,
  ...
}

...

export type ItemPurchaseDataStarted =
  | CancellationInsuranceStarted
  | NewItemStarted
  ...

...
```

tsc will tell you which unions you need to care about, but here are some basic principles:

- If you are modifying a function that deals with a specific type of `PurchasedCartItem`, e.g. [IssueHepstarPolicy.execute](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/10cc7728db25b126f88322f6a53e5b110273f610/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/domain/service/item-update-strategies/issue-hepstar-policy.ts#L25), you will likely need to modify one of the `Hepstar/Busbud/etc.` unions to make it compile. If you are adding a new function that deals with a new and specific type of `PurchasedCartItem`, you will likely need to add a new `Hepstar/Busbud/etc.` style union.
- If you are adding or modifying a function that deals with `PurchasedCartItem`s in general, e.g. [BookPurchasedCartItems.forCart](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/10cc7728db25b126f88322f6a53e5b110273f610/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/application/service/book-purchased-cart-items.ts#L16), you will likely need to modify one or more of the `Started/Failed/etc.` unions to make it compile.
- If you are adding or modifying a function with `Ancillary.PurchasedCartItemResponse` as input or output, e.g. [toLineItems](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/10cc7728db25b126f88322f6a53e5b110273f610/apps/bookie/components/cart-booking-common/ancillary/purchase-line-items.ts#L42) from `ancillary/purchase-line-items.ts`, you will likely need to modify the top level `ItemPurchaseData` union to make it compile.

## `assertNever`

When implementing support for Hepstar, we use a `switch` pattern to explicitly check we have handled all possible item types throughout the code, so that `tsc` will tell us if we've forgotten to define how to handle one or more types. The pattern looks a bit like this:

```tsx
switch (item_purchase_data.type) {
  case ItemDataType.BUSBUD_CANCELLATION_PLAN:
    return this.book_busbud_cancellation_plan.execute();
  case ItemDataType.HEPSTAR_CANCELLATION_INSURANCE:
    return this.issue_hepstar_policy.execute();
  default:
    return assertNever(item_purchase_data);
}

function assertNever(item_purchase_data: never): never {
  throw new BusbudErrors.Impossible(
    `Unknown data type for purchased cart item data ${JSON.stringify(item_purchase_data)}`
  );
}
```

This helps you when adding a new item, as `tsc` will light up in these spots with an error like the following `[tsserver 2345] [E] Argument of type 'NewItemData' is not assignable to parameter of type 'never'.` i.e. 'You have defined a new item type, now you need to define how to handle it.'

At this point, `tsc` is your friend, as it will guide you through all the places you need to support this new `type` in our codebase. If your `ItemPurchaseData` schema hasn't changed, this could be as simple as reusing the handler function for an existing `type`:

```tsx
switch (item_purchase_data.type) {
  case ItemDataType.BUSBUD_CANCELLATION_PLAN:
    return this.book_busbud_cancellation_plan.execute();
  case ItemDataType.HEPSTAR_CANCELLATION_INSURANCE:
  case ItemDataType.NEW_HEPSTAR_ITEM:
    return this.issue_hepstar_policy.execute();
  default:
    return assertNever(item_purchase_data);
}
```

If you needed to add a new `ItemPurchaseData` schema, it will probably require a new handler:

```tsx
switch (item_purchase_data.type) {
  case ItemDataType.BUSBUD_CANCELLATION_PLAN:
    return this.book_busbud_cancellation_plan.execute();
  case ItemDataType.HEPSTAR_CANCELLATION_INSURANCE:
    return this.issue_hepstar_policy.execute();
  case ItemDataType.NEW_HEPSTAR_ITEM:
    return this.new_handler.execute();
  default:
    return assertNever(item_purchase_data);
}
```

At time of writing there is only one place where you'll need to make this sort of change for a `CartItem`—where you define how to transform it into a [PurchasedCartItem](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/10cc7728db25b126f88322f6a53e5b110273f610/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/domain/model/purchased-cart-item.ts#L100) when a user selects it and checks out. But this strategy is then used regularly to process `PurchasedCartItem`s.

## PurchasedCartItem

Now that you're familiar with `ItemPurchaseData` and `assertNever`, here are some of the places tsc will ask you for new information:

- [booking](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/cf6b2db79bc1b7c2fccdf7bacb7ea46f69766c3c/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/application/service/book-purchased-cart-items.ts) (handler typings + `Completed` and `Failed` typings)
- [failure (booking was not performed)](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/cf6b2db79bc1b7c2fccdf7bacb7ea46f69766c3c/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/application/service/fail-incomplete-purchased-cart-items.ts) (handler typings + `Failed` typing)
- [cancellation](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/cf6b2db79bc1b7c2fccdf7bacb7ea46f69766c3c/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/application/service/cancel-completed-purchased-cart-items.ts) (handler typings + `Cancelled` and `CancellationError` typings)
- [validate](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/cf6b2db79bc1b7c2fccdf7bacb7ea46f69766c3c/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/infrastructure/repository/sql-purchased-cart-item-repository.ts#L100) (zod schemas for all the possible states of your data when reading from the db)
- [refundability](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/cf6b2db79bc1b7c2fccdf7bacb7ea46f69766c3c/apps/bookie/components/cart-booking-common/ancillary/purchase-line-items.ts#L92) of/[commission](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/cf6b2db79bc1b7c2fccdf7bacb7ea46f69766c3c/apps/bookie/components/cart-booking-common/ancillary/commissions.ts#L5) (definitions for the refundability and commission earned on items of your new type)

This is the most time-consuming part of adding a new item, but it's also probably the simplest: follow the type errors and fix them. Once everything is green, you've successfully added support for the new item to bookie 🎊.

## Downstream changes

Once support for the new item is added to bookie, you'll need to tell downstream services about it too:

### Hodor:

Add support for displaying the new item on customer receipts. This might not be necessary, but if you've introduced any kind of schema variant it's worth checking hodor can handle it.

It is a pretty simple process, here is an example PR: [https://github.com/busbud/hodor/pull/281](https://github.com/busbud/hodor/pull/281). The relevant code is here:

```html
<if(purchased_item.category === "CANCELLATION_PLAN")>
  <code>${translator.t("hodor.customer-receipt.transaction.cancellation-plan")}</code>
</if>
<if(purchased_item.category === "HEPSTAR")>
  <code>${purchased_item.item_purchase_data.checkout_display.display_data.productname}</code>
</if>
```

### Pubweb:

Similarly to hodor, we want to make sure your new item is labelled correctly.

If you have defined a completely new schema, you will also need to map its data to the `CartItem` component (or a new component if you will be displaying this item using a completely new design).

The simplest place to start is to find the latest [ItemData](https://github.com/busbud/public-website/blob/3fc0e6a23fbc6bf1340bb600388f77ae0447db6d/app/types/ancillary.d.ts#L48-L50) schema and update it if necessary. Then you can update all the locations that depend on this data. At time of writing there are a few different places this data is used that you should check:

Checkout page:

- [CartItem](https://github.com/busbud/public-website/blob/b53d3d23c28ab05af6d59a7d40be4b6d668b6473/app/modules/checkout/components/ancillary/hepstar/cart-item.tsx)
- [CartItemList](https://github.com/busbud/public-website/blob/b53d3d23c28ab05af6d59a7d40be4b6d668b6473/app/modules/checkout/components/ancillary/hepstar/cart-item-list.tsx)
- [CartItemSummaryList](https://github.com/busbud/public-website/blob/b53d3d23c28ab05af6d59a7d40be4b6d668b6473/app/modules/checkout/components/ancillary/cart-item-summary-list.tsx)

Confirmation page:

- [PriceSummary](https://github.com/busbud/public-website/blob/3fc0e6a23fbc6bf1340bb600388f77ae0447db6d/app/modules/purchase-confirmation/components/price-summary/purchased-items.tsx#L47-L48)

Once you think you are done, try purchasing your new cart item locally. If everything looks good, then you really are done 🎊!

## Cleaning up the Hepstar XP

As usual, when the Hepstar XP is adopted or rejected on pubweb, we'll want to clean up all references to its feature flag, which is `ENABLE_HEPSTAR_ANCILLARY`.

But for this XP, there is one extra step. Cleanup will also be required on bookie, as we currently check for the presence of the XP before reporting on conversion to Hepstar by passing info on active experiments to `transformToPurchase` [here](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/02c86d3075a6780d459ca080d0c15a300a1280fe/apps/bookie/routes/cart/v1/purchase.ts#L303-L311). Once the XP is done, you can stop passing this info and delete all checks for an `enable_hepstar` xp value.

## Cancellation/Refunds

- We attach a refund policy to all hepstar items [here](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/2ff00b6e11b2b48c09961f1317412457a9bb68f5/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/infrastructure/client/hepstar/from-response.ts#L106). We should probably generalize this to all cart items, not just the hepstar item purchase data—it's a refactoring project that will make the codebase cleaner in the refund calculation service, e.g. [here](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/2ff00b6e11b2b48c09961f1317412457a9bb68f5/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/application/service/get-purchased-cart-item-refunds.ts#L37-L41).
- We have a service for [calculating refunds](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/2ff00b6e11b2b48c09961f1317412457a9bb68f5/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/application/service/get-purchased-cart-item-refunds.ts#L13). We can use this for displaying information on the refunds available on a subset of ancillary items without actually processing the refund.
- We have a service for [performing refunds](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/2ff00b6e11b2b48c09961f1317412457a9bb68f5/apps/bookie/components/purchase-manager/ancillary/refund/transaction-refund-service.ts#L20). This leverages the previous service to calculate the items/amounts that are refundable, then actually perform the refund and update our records.
- We have a service for [technical cancellations](https://github.com/busbud/napi/blob/2ff00b6e11b2b48c09961f1317412457a9bb68f5/apps/bookie/components/ancillary/application/service/cancel-completed-purchased-cart-items.ts#L17) that will cancel **any ancillary item** regardless of whether the terms allow a refund. This should only be used immediately following a failed payment capture, otherwise our partners will not be happy and we will probably owe them money.

At time of writing only the technical cancellation service is integrated and called in production.

Whenever launching a new hepstar item, remember to define the refund policy, and to make sure CX have the information required to help users who have purchased the item with their questions and requests.

This part of the stack is likely to change, but the above is the present situation at time of writing.

# Infrastructure

Here you’ll find short descriptions and documentation references to all the pieces to our infrastructure and tooling, and the choices that were made along the way.

## What is Docker?

[Docker](https://www.docker.com/) is a piece of software that allows us to package our applications in to image that’s reproducible and deployable to any infrastructure that supports Docker no matter the cloud provider. Imagine it’s like creating a ZIP file of our app and all it’s dependencies that will run the same no matter where you deploy it. [Read more](https://www.docker.com/what-container).

## What is Kubernetes?

[Kubernetes](https://kubernetes.io/) is a platform for managing Docker containers in high work load environments. It’s used within a cluster of virtual machines to give our dockerized applications high availability in case of error or unexpected downtime due to external sources. For example if one of three virtual machines were to die due to unknown causes, Kubernetes would handle moving the applications that were running on that virtual machine to one that’s available until the broken machine recovers. Kubernetes also provides scaling (running more than one instance of your app), [load balancing](https://www.nginx.com/resources/glossary/load-balancing/) (between instances of your app), monitoring, logging, and tight integrations with cloud providers. It’s become increasingly popular in the industry being used by companies such as GitHub and Spotify. [Read more](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/what-is-kubernetes/).

## What is Google Cloud?

[Google Cloud](https://cloud.google.com/) is a cloud infrastructure and tooling provider, most famous for offering virtual machines (servers) for rent. Google Cloud is similar [Amazon’s AWS](https://aws.amazon.com/) or [Microsoft’s Azure](https://azure.microsoft.com/en-ca/), but offers better pricing compared to its competitors, and was first to have a well built Kubernetes integration (see GKE below). [Read More](https://cloud.google.com/).

## What is Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)?

[Google Kubernetes Engine](https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/) is Google Cloud’s Kubernetes integration with their platform, allowing to have access to configuring resources such as load balancers and networking from within the Kubernetes APIs rather then through Google’s. It also provides [UI consoles](https://console.cloud.google.com/kubernetes/workload?project=busbud-integrations) for each of our applications that run within our clusters to show off health status, CPU/memory, scale count, etc. [Read more](https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/).

## What is Terraform?

[Terraform](https://www.terraform.io/intro/index.html) is a tool for building, changing, and versioning infrastructure safely and efficiently with code. Terraform can manage existing and popular service providers such as Google Cloud, AWS, Kubernetes, Datadog, Pingdom, and more! It allows us to write infrastructure as code, and deploy it to several different cloud providers and applications, compared to do it through console UIs or APIs. We mainly use it for deploying our applications on to our Kubernetes cluster using our [terraform module](https://github.com/busbud/integrations/terraform-modules/tree/master/integration). [Read more](https://www.terraform.io/intro/index.html).

## What is Vault?

[Vault](https://www.vaultproject.io/intro/index.html) is a tool for securely accessing secrets. A secret is anything that you want to tightly control access to, such as API keys, passwords, or certificates. Vault provides a unified interface to any secret, while providing tight access control and recording a detailed audit log. We use it to store both secrets and environment variables used by each of our applications running on our Kubernetes Cluster. We’ve created our own [Docker image](https://github.com/busbud/busbud-vault) for Vault to simplify it running on Heroku. [Read more](https://www.vaultproject.io/intro/index.html). You can access vault from the CLI or from [Vault UI](https://vault.busbud-int.com/ui).

## What is AWS Route 53?

[AWS Route 53](https://aws.amazon.com/route53/) is Amazon’s highly scalable DNS provider. Busbud uses them as their default DNS provider for all domains. [Read more](https://aws.amazon.com/route53/).

## What is gcs-logs?

**gcs-logs** is a virtual machine on [Google Compute](https://cloud.google.com/compute/) that’s used to access our logs stored on [Google Cloud Storage](https://cloud.google.com/storage/) with unix tooling. [Read more](../Keeping the Lights On/Maintenance.md).

## What is our Terraform module for applications?

We have our own Terraform module [kube-deployment-route53](https://github.com/busbud/terraform-modules/tree/master/v2/modules/kube-deployment-route53) to easily set configuration for getting any of our applications up and accessible from the internet, connecting all the pieces together such as Kubernetes resources, TLS certificates with Lets Encrypt, Route 53 entries, scaling settings, secrets/environment variables from Vault, and monitoring. All of these resources get grouped together in a Kubernetes Application to make it easy to see at a top level with a [UI](https://console.cloud.google.com/kubernetes/application?project=busbud-integrations).

Here’s a quick overview what each component does. For more details, see the [Terraform module docs](https://github.com/busbud/terraform-modules/tree/master/v2/modules/kube-deployment-route53).

- [Docker Container](https://www.docker.com/what-container) runs our reproducible Docker image in its own isolated environment.
- [Kubernetes Pod](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod/#what-is-a-pod) is a defined group of Docker containers that’s managed by a Replica Set. Anytime a replica set is changed, the pods will be marked for deletion after the new pods come online. If the new pods fail to boot, the old ones remain and the deployment fails. The Kubernetes Service load balances it’s traffic between the running pods if you have more then one instance of your application running (we generally scale to 2 for integrations for example).
- [Kubernetes Replica Set](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/replicaset/) defines a specific version of our application. Every time a new version of your app is deployed, an environment variable changed, or CPU/memory limits modified, a new replica set is created with your intended changes. You can think of them the same as [Heroku releases](https://devcenter.heroku.com/articles/releases).
- [Kubernetes Deployment](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/) manages the state of our application, including the environment variables, CPU and memory restrictions, and managing replica sets.
- [Kubernetes ConfigMap](https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-pod-configmap/) is a version controlled environment variables resource for Kubernetes. Every time an environment variable is changed within Vault, a new ConfigMap is created and assigned to the Kubernetes Deployment in order to trigger a new ReplicaSet and new set of Pods.
- [Kubernetes Secret](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/) are used to store sensitive data that’s used by other Kubernetes resources within the same namespace. The module currently only uses them to store SSL certs for the Kubernetes Ingress.
- [Kubernetes Service](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/) is the dedicated internal load balancer for our application within our cluster, allowing us to run more than one instance of our app and spread traffic between them.
- [Kubernetes Ingress](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/ingress/#what-is-ingress) goes hand in hand with Google’s Load Balancer. Whenever an Ingress is created on our cluster, a Google Load Balancer is created along with it so the Ingress can receive traffic from outside the network.
- [ingress-helper component](https://github.com/busbud/terraform-modules/tree/master/v2/components/kube-ingress-helper) is a hacky workaround for Kubernetes Ingress resources not waiting for IP’s to be assigned to Google’s load balancer. After a Kubernetes Ingress is created, this module will loop and wait for an IP to be assigned before attempting to update Route 53 entries. It also configures the HTTP timeout settings for [Google Backend Services](https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/load-balancing/http/backend-service#timeout-setting) and [RPS per instance](https://cloud.google.com/solutions/scalable-and-resilient-apps#requests_per_second_rps).
- [Google Load Balancer](https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/http-balancer) deploys load balancers on to Google’s edge network (which is outside our cluster and lives all over the world) to point traffic to our cluster and ingress. It also handles SSL termination to reduce latency.
- [AWS Route53](https://aws.amazon.com/route53/) is our DNS provider for domains such as `busbud-int.com`. See [What is Route 53](about:blank#what-is-route-53) for more details.
- [Kubernetes Namespace](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/namespaces/) is used to scope your app. Think of it as all your Terraform resources living in their own folder, separate from every other application on the cluster.
- [Kubernetes Application](https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/application) is used to group multiple Kubernetes resources together that are related. For example, most applications will have a Deployment, an Ingress, and a Configmap. A Kubernetes Application gives you a single spot to view all these resources in a [UI](https://console.cloud.google.com/kubernetes/application?project=busbud-integrations). See [Dora](https://console.cloud.google.com/kubernetes/application/europe-west3-c/busbud-int-eu/dora/dora?project=busbud-integrations) as an example.
- [Google Cluster](https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/) is our virtual machine cluster that runs on [Google Kubernetes Engine](https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/) to provide us the machines to deploy our applications to while keeping them highly available.
- [Pingdom](https://pingdom.com/)

The graph below shows what happens when you run `bb deploy` or `terraform apply` for the first time, the following resources are created in parallel according to their dependencies.

```mermaid
graph TD;
  Env{Env from Vault} --> ConfigMap[Kubernetes ConfigMap];
  ConfigMap --> Deployment[Kubernetes Deployment];

  Deployment --> ReplicaSet[Kubernetes Replica Set];
  ReplicaSet --> Pods[Kubernetes Pods];
  Pods --> Docker[Docker Container];
  Docker --> App;

  Deployment --> Service[Kubernetes Service];

  TLS{TLS Certs from CertManager} --> Secret[Kubernetes Secret];
  Secret --> Ingress;

  Service --> Ingress[Kubernetes Ingress];
  Ingress --> LoadBalancer[Google Load Balancer];
  LoadBalancer --> ingressip[ingress-helper module];
  ingressip --> DNS[AWS Route 53];
  DNS --> Pingdom[Pingdom];
```

After deployment and your app comes fully online, the network path to reach your app is as follows:

```mermaid
graph TD;
  DNS[AWS Route 53] --> LB[Google Load Balancer];
  LB --> Cluster[Google Cluster];
  Cluster --> Ingress[Kubernetes Ingress];
  Ingress --> Service[Kubernetes Service];
  Service --> Pods[Kubernetes Pods];
  Pods --> Docker[Docker Container];
  Docker --> App;
```

When you update your app, the following configs and states are updated in this order to get your app online:

```mermaid
graph TD;
  Deployment[Updated Kubernetes Deployment] --> ReplicaSet[New Kubernetes Replica Set];
  ReplicaSet --> Pods[New Kubernetes Pods];
  Pods --> Docker[New Docker Container];
  Docker --> App;
```

For working with the cluster and debugging possible issues, see [Maintenance/Kubernetes](../maintenance.md#kubernetes).

## Why not Kubernetes Ingress Controllers?

A [Kubernetes Ingress Controller](https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/ingress/#ingress-controllers) is used to route traffic internally for a cluster, allowing to have only a single Google load balancer compared to one for each application. We’ve had some pretty terrible experiences with this as our previous infrastructure had been using the [Deis router](https://github.com/deis/router) as an ingress controller, which would fail to update route entries, make services randomly 404 on requests, and even take services completely offline. We thought it was a better choice to put the effort on to Google’s infrastructure through their load balancers and internal port routing with Kubernetes.

## Why not Heroku?

Busbud has historically ran the majority of our applications on [Heroku](https://devcenter.heroku.com/articles/request-timeout). The main deal breaker for running integrations on Heroku was the fact their router has a [hard 30 second timeout](https://devcenter.heroku.com/articles/request-timeout) on requests to applications. We have some integrations that can take well over a minute to complete a booking. We decided we preferred using a different cloud provider instead of changing the entire communication stack for integrations.

## Why not Google App Engine?

[Google App Engine](https://cloud.google.com/appengine) is Google’s competitor to Heroku, offering the simplicity to deploy your apps to their infrastructure and they manage the rest. Unfortunately App Engine is nearly [triple the price](https://cloud.google.com/appengine) of Heroku, and is cheaper to run a cluster ourselves.

## Why not AWS?

[AWS](https://aws.amazon.com/) wasn’t chosen for a few reasons. Google is cheaper with their [pricing](https://cloud.google.com/compute/pricing), [AWS Elastic Containers for Kubernetes](https://aws.amazon.com/eks/) is still in preview and not generally available, we don’t want to have to install Kubernetes and manage it ourselves on raw VMs, and worst of all we’ve experienced issues with AWS in the past where VMs would *semi crash* not allowing SSH or HTTP access and taking 15+ minutes to reboot. We don’t want to go through that again.

## Why not AWS ECS?

[AWS Elastic Container Service](https://aws.amazon.com/ecs/) is AWS’s Docker container management service and was created before Kubernetes gained popularity. It unfortunately falls in the set of tools that were poorly done at Amazon with bad UIs, confusing infrastructure, and networking issues.

## Why not Google Cloud DNS?

[Google Cloud DNS](https://cloud.google.com/dns/docs/) is Google’s answer to AWS Route53. They both do the same thing, and we had already been using Route 53 before Cloud DNS was created. There’s no other real reason other then to keep things organized and in one place.

## Why no DevOps?

They’re expensive, and to hit the requirements we want he/she will likely code their way out of a job. Better off just having Dustin do it and deal with him complaining the entire time. In all seriousness however, if we do our DevOps right and treat our infrastructure as code, over time DevOps is primarily a maintenance task and go unnoticed by the majority of people. It should just work.

# PDF Tickets

After a booking is completed, we need to provide users with a ticket so they can board the bus (or train, or van, or shuttle). Some partners provide on their APIs endpoints to get a PDF file, but some partners require us to generate one for them.

We also offer Mobile Wallet Tickets, which is described with more details on the next section.

There are currently three scenarios to handle PDF ticket generation:

1. Partner generated: When the partner always provides PDF files
2. Busbud generated: When the partner doesn’t provide PDF files at all, and we need to generate them
3. Partner partially generated: When the partner only provides PDF files for some bookings, but not for others and we must generate those

# Partner Generated

When the partner API provides us with the PDF file, the adapter must implement `getTickets` which is called after `bookTrip` is complete and is responsible for returning all PDF files for all tickets.

# Busbud Generated

When the partner doesn’t provide us with a way to load a ticket, then we can use [Hodor](https://github.com/busbud/hodor) to generate them.

Hodor can generate tickets out of the box, without any additional configuration. If an adapter doesn’t implement `getTickets` , then Hodor will automatically be used to generate tickets, and it will use the default template which includes some basic trip information (segment’s itinerary, ticket and booking reference, selected seat if available, price, and some others).

### QR Code

Hodor tickets can also include a QR Code automatically, as long as the `bookTrip` response includes it under `ticket_fields`.  (example below).

Note that PDF tickets only support “qr” codes.

### Custom fields

Hodor can also automatically include custom fields (https://github.com/busbud/integration-packages/blob/c838db4f8b838c5c02f4c4451716534a90773f92/packages/integration-schemas/schemas/ticket-fields.ts#L27). Note that this is currently only available for v1 tickets, not for redesigned tickets.

### Example response from `bookTrip`:

Below is an example response (from Bluebus) that includes both the QR Code and custom tickets, and uses the default v1 ticket:

 https://github.com/busbud/integrations/blob/7e517770dd908b046677acc907d3c3c32de0ecf9/bluebus/src/adapter/services/book.ts#L100-L105

```tsx
return passengers.reduce((passenger_data, passenger, index) => {
      passenger_data[passenger.id] = {
        tickets: [
          {
            segment_xids: trip.segments.map((segment) => segment.xid),
            reference: "ref",
            booking_reference: "ref",
            reserved_seats: { },
            ticket_fields: {
              barcode: {
                format: "qr",
                message: ticket.qr_code_name,
                alt_text: ticket.qr_code_name
              },
              custom_fields: [
                {
                  key: "trip_number",
                  value: `${trip_number}`,
                  label: "hodor.source.ado.trip-number"
                },
                {
                  key: "seat_type",
                  value: seat_type_name,
                  label: "hodor.field-title.seat-type"
                }
              ]
            },
            details: {}
          }
        ]
      };
      return passenger_data;
    }, {} as UDM.BookTripResponse);
```

### Custom Templates

In some cases, partners may require either a specific design or some information that cannot be generated using the default ticket. For that, Hodor supports creating custom templates for a source. 

The implementation details are described on Hodor ([https://github.com/busbud/hodor?tab=readme-ov-file#adding-a-new-ticket-template](https://github.com/busbud/hodor?tab=readme-ov-file#adding-a-new-ticket-template)).

<aside>
❗ **Important!** You also need to update the source entry on Bookie’s and Grand Central’s database with the default `ticket_template`:

```sql
BEGIN;
UPDATE sources SET ticket_template='template-newsource' WHERE id=<SOURCE_ID>;
COMMIT;
```

</aside>

# Partner Partially Generated

In this case, adapters also need to implement `getTickets` to return PDF files for tickets that support it. 

To control which tickets should be generated using Hodor or the Adapter, a flag `external_pdf` should be used under `ticket.details` on the `bookTrip` response (https://github.com/busbud/integration-packages/blob/2f0710d55af540005ca066c1ec59f33ba7b95647/packages/integration-schemas/udm/schemas/booking/book-trip-response.ts#L34-L38). 

When `external_pdf` is `true` for a given ticket, the adapter will be called to generate the ticket. When `external_pdf` is `false`, then Hodor will be used to generate the ticket.

